var __index = {"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"index.html","title":"DXSuite Help","text":"<p>Welcome to the DXSuite documentation. DXSuite is a digital signage management platform for designing, scheduling, deploying, and monitoring content across distributed display networks.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#quick-links","title":"Quick links","text":"I want to... Go to Log in for the first time First Login Understand the layout of the app Navigating DXSuite Set up locations and displays Locations Schedule content to displays Scheduling and Media Push an urgent message to screens Overrides Review and approve pending content Approvals Manage airport ticket counters Counter Scheduler Troubleshoot a problem Common Issues"},{"location":"index.html#how-this-documentation-is-organized","title":"How this documentation is organized","text":"<ul> <li>Getting Started \u2014 First-time login, orientation, and how tenants and locations fit together</li> <li>Director \u2014 Dashboards, content scheduling, overrides, approvals, and the counter scheduler</li> <li>Designs \u2014 Creating and cataloging display designs and their requirements</li> <li>Locations, Displays and Devices \u2014 The core inventory: sites, screens, and the players that drive them</li> <li>Screenshots \u2014 Viewing live device screenshots</li> <li>Pulse \u2014 At-a-glance health monitoring</li> <li>Connectors and Integrations \u2014 Integrating with external platforms such as Omnivex Ink</li> <li>Configuration \u2014 Reference data (airlines, cities) and content metadata</li> <li>Administration \u2014 Tenants, users and roles, audit history, and platform elevation</li> <li>Troubleshooting \u2014 Solutions to common problems</li> </ul> <p>Which version am I on?</p> <p>Your DXSuite version is shown in the application's Settings area. Documentation reflects the current release; if a screen differs from what you see, check with your administrator about your deployment's version.</p>"},{"location":"administration/audit-log.html","title":"Audit Log","text":"<p>Review a chronological record of platform-level actions, including elevation requests, approvals, and the access operators make to tenants.</p>"},{"location":"administration/audit-log.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<p>You must be a Platform Operator or hold the Auditor role. Auditors can read the audit log across all tenants but have no write access.</p>"},{"location":"administration/audit-log.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>In the left navigation, open Platform \u2192 Audit Log.</li> <li>Review the entries. Each row shows:<ul> <li>Time (UTC) \u2014 when the event occurred.</li> <li>Event \u2014 the type of action (for example, an elevation being requested, approved, or used).</li> <li>Tenant \u2014 the tenant the event relates to, if any.</li> <li>User \u2014 a short identifier for the operator involved.</li> <li>Reason \u2014 the reason or ticket context recorded with the event.</li> </ul> </li> <li>To narrow the list, type into Filter by tenant to show only events for a matching tenant.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"administration/audit-log.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>You see the most recent platform events, newest first. The page loads a capped number of the latest entries, so use the tenant filter to focus on a specific organization.</p> <p>Where else audit events appear</p> <p>A tenant's detail page (Platform \u2192 Tenants \u2192 View) shows the Recent Audit Events for just that tenant, which is useful when investigating a single organization.</p>"},{"location":"administration/audit-log.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Elevation activity is recorded automatically: requesting, approving, and every use of a live elevation against a tenant each write their own entry. See Platform Elevation for how that flow works.</li> <li>Times are shown in UTC to keep records consistent across regions.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"administration/audit-log.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Platform Elevation</li> <li>Tenants</li> </ul>"},{"location":"administration/elevation.html","title":"Platform Elevation","text":"<p>Request temporary, approved access to a tenant you do not normally belong to, so support work is time-bound, justified, and fully audited.</p>"},{"location":"administration/elevation.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>You must be a Platform Operator.</li> <li>A second operator is required to approve your request \u2014 you cannot approve your own.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"administration/elevation.html#how-elevation-works","title":"How elevation works","text":"<p>Platform operators do not have standing access to tenant data. To work inside a tenant they are not a member of, an operator requests an elevation: a time-limited grant for a specific tenant at a chosen access level. Another operator must approve it before it becomes active.</p> <p>Once active, the elevation is enforced automatically for that tenant:</p> <ul> <li>Read Only (TenantSupport) allows viewing tenant data.</li> <li>Read + Write (TenantSupportWrite) is required for any action that changes data. A read-only elevation cannot perform write actions.</li> </ul> <p>Access ends when the elevation expires, and every use of a live elevation is written to the Audit Log.</p>"},{"location":"administration/elevation.html#steps-request-an-elevation","title":"Steps: request an elevation","text":"<ol> <li>In the left navigation, open Platform \u2192 Elevation.</li> <li>Choose the Target Tenant.</li> <li>Choose the Access Level \u2014 Read Only (TenantSupport) or Read + Write (TenantSupportWrite).</li> <li>Set the Duration (hours) (1\u20138).</li> <li>Optionally enter a Ticket Reference (for example, <code>SUPPORT-1234</code>).</li> <li>Enter a Reason. This is required.</li> <li>Select Submit Request.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"administration/elevation.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>Your request is submitted and waits for approval. A second operator reviews pending requests and approves them; they cannot approve a request they made themselves. After approval, your access to the target tenant is live until the duration you requested elapses.</p> <p>Least privilege and shortest duration</p> <p>Request Read Only unless you genuinely need to make changes, and choose the shortest duration that covers the task. Always record a reason (and a ticket reference where one exists) so the access is traceable.</p> <p>Operators who are tenant members</p> <p>If an operator already has an active membership in a tenant, they access it as a normal user and do not need an elevation for that tenant.</p>"},{"location":"administration/elevation.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Audit Log</li> <li>Tenants</li> <li>Users and Roles</li> </ul>"},{"location":"administration/tenants.html","title":"Tenants","text":"<p>Create and manage the tenants (organizations) that share a multi-tenant DXSuite deployment.</p>"},{"location":"administration/tenants.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>You must be a Platform Operator. Other users cannot see the Platform area.</li> <li>A tenant registry database must be configured for the deployment. On standalone or on-premises installations the Platform area is not shown, and provisioning is unavailable.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"administration/tenants.html#view-tenants","title":"View tenants","text":"<ol> <li>In the left navigation, open Platform \u2192 Tenants.</li> <li>The list shows each tenant's Tenant ID, Display Name, Subdomain, and Status (Active or Disabled).</li> <li>Select View on a row to open the tenant's detail page.</li> </ol> <p>The detail page shows the tenant's core properties along with its Users, Recent Elevations, and Recent Audit Events.</p>"},{"location":"administration/tenants.html#steps-create-a-tenant","title":"Steps: create a tenant","text":"<ol> <li>On the Tenants page, select + New Tenant.</li> <li>Complete the form:<ul> <li>Tenant ID \u2014 a unique slug (for example, <code>acme</code>).</li> <li>Display Name \u2014 the organization's name as shown in DXSuite.</li> <li>Subdomain \u2014 the subdomain used to reach the tenant.</li> <li>Customer Code \u2014 your internal customer identifier.</li> <li>Director Connection String \u2014 the connection to the tenant's Director database.</li> <li>Design Connection String \u2014 optional; leave blank if Design is not licensed for this tenant.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Select Create Tenant.</li> <li>In the Confirm Privileged Action dialog, select Confirm.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"administration/tenants.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>The new tenant is provisioned and you are taken to its detail page. Tenant ID, Display Name, Subdomain, and Customer Code are all required \u2014 if any is missing, DXSuite reports an error and no tenant is created.</p>"},{"location":"administration/tenants.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>To change a tenant's properties later, open its detail page and select Edit.</li> <li>A tenant only exposes the modules and features it is licensed for. See Tenants and Locations for how tenants scope everything a user sees.</li> </ul> <p>Connection strings point to real databases</p> <p>The Director and Design connection strings determine which databases the tenant reads and writes. Double-check them before confirming \u2014 an incorrect value can leave the tenant unable to load its data.</p>"},{"location":"administration/tenants.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Users and Roles</li> <li>Platform Elevation</li> <li>Audit Log</li> </ul>"},{"location":"administration/users-and-roles.html","title":"Users and Roles","text":"<p>Manage who can sign in to DXSuite and what each person is allowed to do, using roles, groups, and \u2014 in multi-tenant deployments \u2014 platform-level operator accounts.</p>"},{"location":"administration/users-and-roles.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>To manage users and groups, you need access to the Users and Groups settings tabs.</li> <li>To create or edit roles, you must have the Admin role. Only administrators can manage roles.</li> <li>To manage platform users and their tenant memberships, you must be a Platform Operator.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"administration/users-and-roles.html#roles","title":"Roles","text":"<p>Every user is assigned one role, which controls the tabs, pages, and features they can reach. DXSuite ships with these built-in roles:</p> <ul> <li>Admin \u2014 full access to everything, including role management. Admin bypasses all permission checks.</li> <li>Architect \u2014 broad configuration access.</li> <li>Contributor \u2014 day-to-day content work.</li> <li>New User \u2014 minimal default access.</li> </ul> <p>You can also create custom roles. A custom role inherits from a built-in base role, then adds or removes access through explicit grants for settings tabs, configuration tabs, and feature permissions (such as Content Upload, Content Edit, and Content Delete).</p>"},{"location":"administration/users-and-roles.html#steps-create-a-custom-role","title":"Steps: create a custom role","text":"<ol> <li>Open Settings \u2192 Roles.</li> <li>Select Add Role.</li> <li>Enter a Role Name and Description, and choose a Base Role.</li> <li>Check the Settings Tabs, Configuration Tabs, and Feature Permissions the role should grant.</li> <li>Select Save.</li> </ol> <p>Content still respects location access</p> <p>Content feature permissions are additionally limited by each user's location access level, so granting a permission does not override the locations a user is scoped to.</p>"},{"location":"administration/users-and-roles.html#users","title":"Users","text":"<ol> <li>Open Settings \u2192 Users.</li> <li>Search for and select a user to open their details.</li> <li>From here you can:<ul> <li>Set the user's Current Role and select Save.</li> <li>Add or remove group assignments.</li> <li>Set a default Landing Page.</li> <li>Disable/Enable or Delete the account, or Reset Password for local accounts.</li> </ul> </li> </ol> <p>Where local accounts are enabled, use + Create Local Account to add a user with an email and password.</p>"},{"location":"administration/users-and-roles.html#groups","title":"Groups","text":"<p>Open Settings \u2192 Groups to create groups and manage their members. Groups let you organize users; directory-synced groups are managed by the sync process and are marked accordingly.</p>"},{"location":"administration/users-and-roles.html#platform-users-multi-tenant","title":"Platform users (multi-tenant)","text":"<p>In multi-tenant deployments, Platform \u2192 Users lists platform-level accounts. For each user you can see whether they are a Platform Operator and how many tenants they belong to. Open a user to manage their Tenant Memberships \u2014 activate or deactivate a membership, or grant or revoke Tenant Admin. These changes require confirming a privileged-action prompt.</p>"},{"location":"administration/users-and-roles.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Tenants</li> <li>Platform Elevation</li> <li>First Login</li> </ul>"},{"location":"configuration/airlines-cities.html","title":"Airlines and Cities","text":"<p>Maintain the airline and city reference data that aviation displays rely on, using the Airlines and Cities tabs in the Configuration area.</p>"},{"location":"configuration/airlines-cities.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>Permission to open Configuration.</li> <li>The Aviation module enabled for your tenant. If you do not see the Airlines and Cities tabs, the module is not available in this tenant.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"configuration/airlines-cities.html#airlines","title":"Airlines","text":"<p>The Airlines tab links each airline to the airport locations it operates from, and manages the wayfinding PIN for airlines that use one.</p>"},{"location":"configuration/airlines-cities.html#steps-assign-locations-to-an-airline","title":"Steps: assign locations to an airline","text":"<ol> <li>Open Configuration and select the Airlines tab.</li> <li>In Search Airline, start typing an airline name or code, then pick the airline. Its Airline Code is shown alongside.</li> <li>In Search IATA Locations, start typing a location name or code and pick a match.</li> <li>Select Add. The location appears under Assigned Locations.</li> <li>To remove a link, select Remove next to a location.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"configuration/airlines-cities.html#steps-update-a-wayfinding-pin","title":"Steps: update a wayfinding PIN","text":"<ol> <li>Select an airline that uses a PIN. The Wayfinding PIN field shows the current value.</li> <li>In Update PIN, enter a new 4-digit PIN.</li> <li>Select Save.</li> </ol> <p>Note</p> <p>The PIN fields appear only for airlines configured to use a wayfinding PIN.</p>"},{"location":"configuration/airlines-cities.html#cities","title":"Cities","text":"<p>The Cities tab links each city (airport location) to the languages its content should be available in.</p>"},{"location":"configuration/airlines-cities.html#steps-assign-languages-to-a-city","title":"Steps: assign languages to a city","text":"<ol> <li>Open Configuration and select the Cities tab.</li> <li>In Search City, start typing a city name or IATA code (at least three characters), then pick the city. Its Airport Code is shown alongside.</li> <li>In Search Languages, start typing a language name and pick a match.</li> <li>Select Add. The language appears under Assigned Languages.</li> <li>To remove a link, select Remove next to a language.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"configuration/airlines-cities.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>Airlines carry their assigned locations and PIN, and cities carry their assigned languages. This reference data is shared across the tenant and used by aviation displays and scheduling.</p>"},{"location":"configuration/airlines-cities.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Content Metadata</li> <li>Locations</li> </ul>"},{"location":"configuration/metadata.html","title":"Content Metadata","text":"<p>Metadata keys are custom fields you define once and then use to tag devices, content, and access rules \u2014 the basis for filtering content to the right locations.</p>"},{"location":"configuration/metadata.html#what-a-metadata-key-is","title":"What a metadata key is","text":"<p>A metadata key is a reusable field with a name and a type. After you define a key, its values can be applied across DXSuite \u2014 for example, tagging a media asset with a Department or a Region \u2014 so content can be matched to the locations and audiences it belongs to.</p> <p>Metadata keys are managed on the Metadata tab of the Configuration area, shown as a list of Metadata Keys.</p>"},{"location":"configuration/metadata.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>Permission to open Configuration.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"configuration/metadata.html#steps-add-a-metadata-key","title":"Steps: add a metadata key","text":"<ol> <li>Open Configuration and select the Metadata tab.</li> <li>Select + Add Key.</li> <li>Enter the Key \u2014 the internal identifier (for example, <code>department</code>).</li> <li>Enter a Display Name shown to users (for example, <code>Department</code>).</li> <li>Choose a Field Type:<ul> <li>String \u2014 free text.</li> <li>Int \u2014 a whole number.</li> <li>List \u2014 a single choice from options you define.</li> <li>Multi-Select \u2014 one or more choices from options you define.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Optionally add a Description to guide whoever fills in the value.</li> <li>For List or Multi-Select, select + Add Option to define each choice.</li> <li>Select Save.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"configuration/metadata.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>The key appears in the Metadata Keys list with its type and options. It is now available wherever metadata is applied \u2014 including media assets, devices, and access rules \u2014 and the values drive which content is filtered to each location.</p>"},{"location":"configuration/metadata.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>The Key cannot be changed after creation, though its display name, description, and options can be edited.</li> <li>A key that is in use cannot be deleted. DXSuite lists where it is used (devices, users, groups, assets, or access rules) so you can clear those references first.</li> </ul> <p>Consistent choices</p> <p>Prefer List or Multi-Select for values that should be picked from a fixed set. This keeps tagging consistent and makes location filtering reliable.</p>"},{"location":"configuration/metadata.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Airlines and Cities</li> <li>Scheduling and Media</li> <li>Devices</li> </ul>"},{"location":"connectors/omnivex.html","title":"Omnivex Ink Integration","text":"<p>Connect DXSuite to an Omnivex Ink platform so its displays and content folders sync into Director as managed devices and Content Channels.</p>"},{"location":"connectors/omnivex.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>Permission to open Configuration.</li> <li>The base URL of your Omnivex Ink environment.</li> <li>An Omnivex Ink API key with access to the displays and content you want to import.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"connectors/omnivex.html#steps-connect-omnivex-ink","title":"Steps: connect Omnivex Ink","text":"<ol> <li>Open Configuration and select the Cloud Connectors tab.</li> <li>Select + Add Connector.</li> <li>Choose Omnivex Ink from the Platform list.</li> <li>In Display Name, enter a name that identifies this environment (for example, the site or region).</li> <li>Enter the Base URL of your Omnivex Ink environment and paste your API Key.</li> <li>Under Content Mode, choose whether to import content, track device status, or both.</li> <li>Under Import Resources, tick Displays, Folders, or both.</li> <li>Optionally set a Credential Expiration date.</li> <li>Select SAVE.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"connectors/omnivex.html#steps-verify-and-sync","title":"Steps: verify and sync","text":"<ol> <li>On the connector card, select TEST CONNECTION and confirm it reports Connected.</li> <li>Select SYNC INVENTORY to import from Omnivex Ink.</li> <li>Review the result message showing how many displays and folders were brought in.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"connectors/omnivex.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>Omnivex Ink displays appear in Device Management, and imported folders appear as Content Channels you can schedule against. The card shows the last sync time and status each time you run a sync.</p> <p>Test before syncing</p> <p>If TEST CONNECTION reports Failed, check the base URL and API key before syncing. A failed sync is recorded on the card so you can see what went wrong.</p>"},{"location":"connectors/omnivex.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>If folders do not import, confirm the API key includes access to content, then sync again.</li> <li>Re-run SYNC INVENTORY after adding displays or folders in Omnivex Ink to bring them into DXSuite.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"connectors/omnivex.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Connector Overview</li> <li>Content Metadata</li> <li>Scheduling and Media</li> </ul>"},{"location":"connectors/overview.html","title":"Connector Overview","text":"<p>Connectors let DXSuite pull devices and content from an external cloud platform into Director, so you can manage them alongside your own displays.</p>"},{"location":"connectors/overview.html#what-a-connector-does","title":"What a connector does","text":"<p>A connector links DXSuite to a third-party cloud CMS or device-management platform (such as Omnivex Ink). Once configured, DXSuite can:</p> <ul> <li>Import devices from the platform, which then appear in Device Management.</li> <li>Import content folders from the platform, which then appear as Content Channels.</li> <li>Report device status back into DXSuite so cloud-managed displays sit next to your own.</li> </ul> <p>Connectors are managed on the Cloud Connectors tab of the Configuration area. Each tenant keeps its own connectors, and you can configure more than one.</p>"},{"location":"connectors/overview.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>Permission to open Configuration.</li> <li>An account on the external platform, its base URL, and an API key with the access you need.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"connectors/overview.html#steps-add-a-connector","title":"Steps: add a connector","text":"<ol> <li>Open Configuration and select the Cloud Connectors tab.</li> <li>Select + Add Connector.</li> <li>Choose the platform from the Platform list. The Display Name fills in automatically \u2014 edit it to something recognizable.</li> <li>Enter the Base URL and API Key for the platform.</li> <li>If the platform supports content import, choose a Content Mode and tick the resources to bring in under Import Resources.</li> <li>Optionally set a Credential Expiration date so DXSuite can warn you before the API key lapses.</li> <li>Select SAVE.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"connectors/overview.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>The connector appears as a card showing its platform, status, base URL, and linked device count. Use TEST CONNECTION to confirm the credentials work, then SYNC INVENTORY to import devices and folders.</p> <p>Re-sync any time</p> <p>Running SYNC INVENTORY again refreshes the imported devices and folders. Items removed on the external platform are cleared from DXSuite on the next sync.</p>"},{"location":"connectors/overview.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Use Enable and Disable to pause a connector without losing its configuration.</li> <li>Delete removes the connector. You are prompted first, with the option to also remove its imported devices and Content Channel folders.</li> <li>A connector whose credential has expired, or expires within two weeks, is flagged on its card.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"connectors/overview.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Omnivex Ink Integration</li> <li>Content Metadata</li> <li>Devices</li> </ul>"},{"location":"designs/catalog.html","title":"Catalog","text":"<p>Browse and maintain the shared reference data that designs draw on when you build worksheets and estimates.</p>"},{"location":"designs/catalog.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>The Designs area must be enabled for your tenant.</li> <li>Catalog data is shared across all designs for your tenant, so changes here affect every design.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"designs/catalog.html#open-the-catalog","title":"Open the Catalog","text":"<p>Select Catalog from the top navigation. The page opens on the Items tab, with the remaining data organized across the tabs below.</p>"},{"location":"designs/catalog.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>Select a tab to browse that part of the catalog:<ul> <li>Items \u2014 Equipment and products pulled from NetSuite, each with a description, manufacturer, and category.</li> <li>Categories \u2014 The categories used to group items, optionally organized into category groups.</li> <li>Labor \u2014 Labor types (roles) and the columns that drive labor calculations.</li> <li>Attributes \u2014 Reusable attributes that describe items and categories.</li> <li>Misc \u2014 Miscellaneous line items.</li> <li>Travel \u2014 Travel cost settings.</li> <li>Materials \u2014 Materials categories and items.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Use the actions within a tab \u2014 typically Add, Edit, Refresh, and Delete \u2014 to maintain its records.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"designs/catalog.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>The catalog reflects your changes immediately, and the updated items, categories, and labor become available when building worksheets in a design.</p>"},{"location":"designs/catalog.html#add-a-catalog-item","title":"Add a catalog item","text":"<ol> <li>On the Items tab, select + Add Item.</li> <li>Search for and select the matching NetSuite item.</li> <li>Save the item. Use Refresh on any existing row later to pull the latest details from NetSuite.</li> </ol> <p>Items come from NetSuite</p> <p>Catalog items are sourced from the connected NetSuite account. If an item cannot be found when adding or refreshing, confirm it exists in NetSuite first.</p>"},{"location":"designs/catalog.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>A category cannot be deleted while items are still assigned to it; reassign or remove those items first.</li> <li>On the Labor tab, drag column rows to set their left-to-right order in the equipment table.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"designs/catalog.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Designs</li> <li>Requirements</li> </ul>"},{"location":"designs/designs.html","title":"Designs","text":"<p>Create and manage display designs, each built from a worksheet and a set of project requirements.</p>"},{"location":"designs/designs.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>The Designs area must be enabled for your tenant. If you do not see it in the top navigation, it is not enabled for your account.</li> <li>To link a design to an opportunity, that opportunity must exist in the connected NetSuite account.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"designs/designs.html#create-a-design","title":"Create a design","text":"<ol> <li>Open Designs from the top navigation.</li> <li>Select New Design.</li> <li>In Search Opportunities, type a customer name or opportunity number and pick a result to link it. To create an unlinked design instead, select Skip (Blank Design).</li> <li>Enter a Design Name. Linked designs are pre-filled with the customer and opportunity number; adjust as needed.</li> <li>Select Create Design.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"designs/designs.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>The design is created with a starter worksheet and the default requirement categories, and it opens ready to edit. Linked designs show an Opp badge with the opportunity number.</p>"},{"location":"designs/designs.html#find-and-open-a-design","title":"Find and open a design","text":"<p>The Designs list shows every design for your tenant, newest first, with its opportunity, customer, status, sales rep, and created date.</p> <ul> <li>Use the search box to match on name, opportunity, customer, or sales rep.</li> <li>Narrow the list with the All Sales Reps and All Customers filters.</li> <li>Select any row to open the design.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"designs/designs.html#edit-a-design","title":"Edit a design","text":"<p>Inside a design you can:</p> <ul> <li>Rename it \u2014 select the design name in the header, type a new name, and press Enter.</li> <li>Switch tabs \u2014 use Worksheet to build the equipment and labor estimate, or Requirements to capture and track project requirements. See Requirements.</li> <li>Manage worksheets \u2014 when more than one exists, use the worksheet selector in the header to switch between them, the pencil to rename the active one, or + New to add another.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"designs/designs.html#delete-a-design","title":"Delete a design","text":"<ol> <li>In the Designs list, select the trash icon on the design's row.</li> <li>Select Delete to confirm, or Cancel to keep it.</li> </ol> <p>Deletion is permanent</p> <p>Deleting a design also removes its worksheets and requirements. This cannot be undone.</p>"},{"location":"designs/designs.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>The item, category, and labor data available in a worksheet comes from the shared Catalog. Set the catalog up first so new designs have everything they need.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"designs/designs.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Catalog</li> <li>Requirements</li> </ul>"},{"location":"designs/requirements.html","title":"Requirements","text":"<p>Capture, organize, review, and export the project requirements for a design.</p>"},{"location":"designs/requirements.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>An existing design. See Designs.</li> <li>Open the design and select the Requirements tab.</li> </ul> <p>Every new design starts with a default set of requirement categories. Categories belong to a single design, so you can tailor them freely without affecting other designs.</p>"},{"location":"designs/requirements.html#add-a-requirement","title":"Add a requirement","text":"<ol> <li>On the Requirements tab, select Add Requirement.</li> <li>Choose a Category.</li> <li>Set a Priority \u2014 Must Have, Should Have, or Nice to Have.</li> <li>Enter a Description, and optional Client Notes.</li> <li>Optionally set Assign To to give the requirement an owner.</li> <li>Select Add Requirement.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"designs/requirements.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>The requirement appears in its category, numbered as category.item (for example 2.1), and starts with a Pending Review status. To change it later, select it and choose Edit.</p>"},{"location":"designs/requirements.html#organize-categories-and-ordering","title":"Organize categories and ordering","text":"<p>Requirements are numbered automatically. Categories that contain at least one requirement are numbered <code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, <code>3</code>\u2026 in order, and requirements within a category are sub-numbered <code>1.1</code>, <code>1.2</code>, <code>2.1</code>, and so on.</p> <ul> <li>Reorder requirements \u2014 drag a card by its handle to move it within its category.</li> <li>Manage categories \u2014 select Manage Categories to add, rename, or delete categories, and drag them to reorder. The category order sets the numbering used both on screen and in the export.</li> </ul> <p>Deleting a category</p> <p>A category can only be deleted once it has no requirements. Move or delete its requirements first.</p>"},{"location":"designs/requirements.html#review-requirements","title":"Review requirements","text":"<p>Expand a requirement card to record a designer decision using Accept, Needs Clarification, or Challenge, and to add comments in the discussion thread. The toolbar shows how many requirements have been accepted, alongside a progress bar. Use the category chips to filter the list to a single category.</p>"},{"location":"designs/requirements.html#export-to-excel","title":"Export to Excel","text":"<ol> <li>Select Export.</li> <li>Choose a version:<ul> <li>Customer version \u2014 the requirement number, description, priority, and client notes.</li> <li>Internal version \u2014 the same columns plus status and assignee.</li> </ul> </li> <li>The workbook downloads as <code>Requirements - &lt;design name&gt;.xlsx</code>.</li> </ol> <p>The export mirrors the on-screen numbering and includes a header block with the design name, opportunity, customer, sales rep, and date.</p>"},{"location":"designs/requirements.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Only categories that contain requirements are numbered and exported; empty categories are skipped.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"designs/requirements.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Designs</li> <li>Catalog</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/approvals.html","title":"Approvals","text":"<p>The Approvals page is where content moves through review before it can be scheduled: reviewers act on items in their queue, and submitters track the status of what they sent.</p>"},{"location":"director/approvals.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>You must either belong to an approval group (to review content) or have submitted content for approval (to track it). Administrators can see all pending items.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/approvals.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":""},{"location":"director/approvals.html#review-items-in-your-queue","title":"Review items in your queue","text":"<ol> <li>Select Approvals in the Director navigation to open CONTENT APPROVALS.</li> <li>Open the My Queue tab. Each row shows a preview, asset name, location, who uploaded it, your group, the current stage, and when it was submitted.</li> <li>For an item, choose an action:<ul> <li>Approve \u2014 move it to the next stage, or complete approval if it is the final stage.</li> <li>Decline \u2014 reject the content.</li> <li>Detail \u2014 view the full stage progression and comment history.</li> </ul> </li> <li>In the action dialog, add notes if needed, then confirm. Your decision and any note are recorded.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"director/approvals.html#track-your-submissions","title":"Track your submissions","text":"<ol> <li>Open the My Submissions tab.</li> <li>Use the Status filter to show All, Pending, Approved, or Declined items.</li> <li>For a declined item, select Re-submit, explain why in the required comment, and confirm to send it back into the approval flow.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"director/approvals.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>Approved content becomes available to schedule on displays. Declined content is held for a limited retention period (shown as an auto-delete countdown) unless you re-submit it.</p> <p>Multi-stage approval</p> <p>Content can pass through several stages, each involving one or more groups. The Detail view shows every stage, which group acted, when, and any notes. The Stage column shows progress as current stage over total stages.</p>"},{"location":"director/approvals.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Administrators also see an All Pending tab listing items awaiting approval across every group, for oversight.</li> <li>An item marked AI Auto was approved automatically by content moderation rather than by a reviewer.</li> <li>Counts on each tab show how many items need your attention.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/approvals.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Scheduling and Media</li> <li>Overrides</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/counter-scheduler.html","title":"Counter Scheduler","text":"<p>The Counter Scheduler assigns airlines to ticket-counter positions on a video wall across the day, and sets the background, mask, and logo content each airline shows at its counters.</p>"},{"location":"director/counter-scheduler.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>The Counter Scheduler feature must be enabled for your site, and you must have permission to access it.</li> <li>A Video Wall location configured with counter groups that allow airline allocation.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/counter-scheduler.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":""},{"location":"director/counter-scheduler.html#schedule-airlines-on-counters","title":"Schedule airlines on counters","text":"<ol> <li>Select Counter Scheduler in the Director navigation. If prompted, choose a Video Wall location from the picker.</li> <li>Use the date controls at the top (the arrows or the date box) to move to the day you want to schedule.</li> <li>On the Counter Schedule tab, select an open time slot on the calendar to create a schedule block. To change an existing block, select it; you can also drag it to move it or drag its edge to resize it.</li> <li>In the block dialog, set:<ul> <li>Airline Group \u2014 the airline assigned to that counter time.</li> <li>Default Asset (optional) \u2014 content that plays automatically when the block becomes active.</li> <li>All Day, or a Start and End time.</li> <li>From date / To date (optional) and Recurs on days. Leave all days unchecked to apply every day.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Select SAVE. If the block overlaps another, a warning appears; select Save Anyway to confirm, or adjust the times.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"director/counter-scheduler.html#set-an-airlines-counter-content","title":"Set an airline's counter content","text":"<ol> <li>Open the Airline Content tab.</li> <li>For each airline card, set the layers you need:<ul> <li>Counter Content \u2014 the default content package for that airline's counters.</li> <li>Background Layer, Mask Layer, and Logo \u2014 provide a web URL or upload a file, and choose a render mode where offered.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Select SAVE on the card.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"director/counter-scheduler.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>Scheduled blocks appear on the calendar for the selected day, colored by airline. When a block is active, its airline (and any default asset) drives the assigned counter displays, using the content configured on the Airline Content tab.</p> <p>Which airlines appear</p> <p>Only groups assigned to this video wall are listed as schedulable airlines. Assignments are configured per group in Settings \u2192 Groups. If an airline is missing, check its group assignment.</p>"},{"location":"director/counter-scheduler.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>A block with no airline set is treated as Unassigned.</li> <li>Overlapping blocks on the same display are blocked when you move or resize them, and warned about when you save.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/counter-scheduler.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Scheduling and Media</li> <li>Locations</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/dashboard.html","title":"Dashboard","text":"<p>The Director dashboard is your starting point: it lists the locations you have access to, grouped for quick browsing, so you can jump straight to the site you want to work on.</p>"},{"location":"director/dashboard.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>Your account must be assigned to at least one location. If none are assigned, the dashboard appears empty.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/dashboard.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>Select Dashboard in the Director navigation. The page opens under the LOCATIONS heading.</li> <li>Browse the location groups. Each group is a collapsible section with its own heading.<ul> <li>Select a group heading (or its chevron) to collapse or expand that group.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Locate the site you want and select its tile. Each tile shows the location image and name.</li> <li>You are taken to that location, where you can drill into its displays and content.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"director/dashboard.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>You land on the selected location's page, from which you can open a display to manage its schedule and content.</p> <p>You only see your locations</p> <p>The dashboard is filtered to the locations assigned to your account. Groups that contain none of your assigned locations are hidden. If a site you expect is missing, ask your administrator to assign it to you.</p>"},{"location":"director/dashboard.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Groups remember their collapsed or expanded state while you stay on the page, so you can tidy the view down to the groups you use most.</li> <li>The dashboard is read-only browsing \u2014 you create and change content once you open a location and one of its displays.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/dashboard.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Scheduling and Media</li> <li>Locations</li> <li>Displays</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/overrides.html","title":"Overrides","text":"<p>An override pushes urgent or special-event content to many devices or displays at once, taking over whatever they are currently showing until you turn it off.</p>"},{"location":"director/overrides.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>Permission to access the Overrides page. The Overrides menu item appears only when the feature is enabled for your site and you are authorized.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/overrides.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":""},{"location":"director/overrides.html#create-an-override","title":"Create an override","text":"<ol> <li>Select Overrides in the Director navigation to open OVERRIDE CONTROL.</li> <li>Select + CREATE OVERRIDE. The override editor opens.</li> <li>Under Basic Information, enter an Override Name (required) and an optional Description.</li> <li>Choose the targets. In Target Selection, switch between Displays and Devices, then search and tick the items to include. Use Select All Filtered, Unselect All, or Show Selected to manage the list.</li> <li>Assign content by resolution. Select Detect Resolutions to list the resolutions of your selected targets, then upload an image or video for each. Use + Add Custom Resolution to add one manually.</li> <li>(Optional) Under Schedule Settings, tick Enable Scheduling and set the Start Date, End Date, Start Time, End Time, and Days of Week. Shortcut buttons set All, Weekdays, Weekends, or Clear.</li> <li>Select SAVE.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"director/overrides.html#activate-and-manage-overrides","title":"Activate and manage overrides","text":"<ol> <li>On OVERRIDE CONTROL, each override appears as a card in the Override Priority List with its device count and status.</li> <li>Use the toggle switch on a card to activate or deactivate it manually.</li> <li>Drag cards by the handle to reorder them. Items higher in the list take priority when more than one override is active for the same device.</li> <li>Use the edit and delete icons to change or remove an override.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"director/overrides.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>While an override is active, its assigned content plays on the targeted displays and devices at the matching resolution. The card shows ACTIVE, and the source of activation is noted as (Manual) or (Schedule).</p> <p>Overrides interrupt normal content</p> <p>An active override replaces scheduled and default content on its targets. Deactivate it (or let its schedule end) to return displays to their normal programming.</p>"},{"location":"director/overrides.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>An override can be activated three ways: the manual toggle, a schedule you set, or an external trigger via the platform API.</li> <li>Deleting an override also removes its device and display associations and its content assignments.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/overrides.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Scheduling and Media</li> <li>Displays</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/scheduling.html","title":"Scheduling and Media","text":"<p>Schedule what plays on a display: set its always-on default content and add timed schedule entries that take over on chosen dates, times, and days of the week.</p>"},{"location":"director/scheduling.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>Access to a location and at least one display within it. Open a display's schedule from its location page \u2014 scheduling is reached from within a display, not from the top navigation.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/scheduling.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>From a location, open the display you want to schedule. The MANAGE SCHEDULE page opens.</li> <li>Review the status banner at the top. It shows whether the display is currently showing override, scheduled, or default content, along with a preview.</li> <li>To add or replace content, select NEW SCHEDULE ENTRY. The Schedule Editor opens.</li> <li>Add the media you want to use with the buttons in Add Media Content:<ul> <li>ADD MEDIA + \u2014 upload an image or video.</li> <li>ADD WEB + \u2014 add a web page URL.</li> <li>ADD STREAM + \u2014 add a live stream URL.</li> <li>CREATE MEDIA + \u2014 build an image in the Media Creator (see below).</li> <li>CREATE TEMPLATE + \u2014 generate content from a template.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Under Available Assets, drag the assets you want onto the Schedule Content area. Each item plays in the order added.</li> <li>For each content item, use Edit Duration to set how long it stays on screen (where applicable).</li> <li>Under Schedule Configuration, choose how the entry applies:<ul> <li>Tick Default Content to make this the display's always-on content instead of a timed entry.</li> <li>For a timed entry, set the Start and End date and time. Leave a date blank for no limit; leave a time blank for all-day.</li> <li>Under Active Days, tick the days it should run. Leave all unticked to run every day.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Select SAVE SCHEDULE ENTRY (or SAVE SCHEDULE EDIT when editing).</li> </ol>"},{"location":"director/scheduling.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>The entry appears under Current Schedule Entries, showing its content, date range, time range, and active days. The display shows scheduled content whenever an entry's date, time, and day conditions all match; otherwise it falls back to the default content.</p> <p>Using the Media Creator</p> <p>CREATE MEDIA + opens the Media Creator, a canvas editor for building an image sized to the display. Add text and elements, position them, then generate the image as a new asset that appears in Available Assets.</p>"},{"location":"director/scheduling.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Edit an existing entry with its Edit button, or remove it with Delete.</li> <li>If content approval is enabled for the location, newly added assets may show an Awaiting Approval or Declined overlay and cannot be scheduled until approved. See Approvals.</li> <li>Use Edit Name near the top of the page to rename the display.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"director/scheduling.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Approvals</li> <li>Overrides</li> <li>Displays</li> </ul>"},{"location":"getting-started/first-login.html","title":"First Login","text":"<p>How to sign in to DXSuite for the first time and confirm your account is ready to use.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started/first-login.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>A DXSuite web address (URL) provided by your administrator.</li> <li>One of the following, depending on how your organization is configured:<ul> <li>A single sign-on (SSO) account \u2014 Microsoft, Google, or Okta.</li> <li>A directory (Active Directory) username and password.</li> <li>A DXSuite email-and-password account.</li> </ul> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"getting-started/first-login.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>Open your DXSuite URL in a web browser. You will land on the sign-in card.</li> <li>Choose the sign-in method your organization uses. The card only shows the options that are enabled for your site:<ul> <li>Sign in with Microsoft / Google / Okta \u2014 select the provider button and complete that provider's sign-in prompt.</li> <li>Sign in with Active Directory \u2014 enter your Username or UPN and Password, then select the sign-in button.</li> <li>Email and password \u2014 enter your Email and Password, then select Sign In.</li> </ul> </li> <li>If email accounts are enabled and you do not have one yet, select Don't have an account? Create one, enter your Email, Password, and Confirm Password, then select Create Account.</li> <li>If you just registered, check your inbox for a confirmation link and confirm your address before signing in.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"getting-started/first-login.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>After signing in you land on the main DXSuite workspace with the top navigation bar visible. Your available modules and menu items reflect your assigned role and your site's enabled features.</p> <p>Verify your account and role</p> <p>Select your profile avatar (the circle with your initials) at the top-right. The menu header shows your name and current role (for example Admin, Architect, or Contributor). A newly registered account may start as New User with limited access until an administrator assigns a role.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started/first-login.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Account passwords must meet your site's complexity rules (minimum length and required character types). If registration is rejected, review the on-screen message.</li> <li>After repeated failed attempts, an account may be temporarily locked. Wait and try again, or contact your administrator.</li> <li>For security, DXSuite signs you out automatically after a period of inactivity. Simply sign in again to continue.</li> </ul> <p>Don't see the option you expected?</p> <p>Sign-in methods are configured per site (and can vary per tenant). If a provider or the email form is missing, your administrator has not enabled it for your account.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started/first-login.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Navigating DXSuite</li> <li>Tenants and Locations</li> </ul>"},{"location":"getting-started/navigation.html","title":"Navigating DXSuite","text":"<p>An orientation to the top navigation bar, which is your starting point for everything in DXSuite.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started/navigation.html#the-top-navigation-bar","title":"The top navigation bar","text":"<p>Once you are signed in, a bar runs across the top of every page. From left to right:</p> <ol> <li>Brand logo \u2014 Select the DXSuite logo on the far left to return to the home page at any time.</li> <li>Product module buttons \u2014 The main work areas (for example Director) appear as buttons next to the logo. Related pages are grouped: a grouped button opens a dropdown of its pages, while a standalone button navigates directly. The active area is highlighted.</li> <li>Tenant switcher \u2014 If your account belongs to more than one tenant, a switcher on the right shows the current tenant's name. See Tenants and Locations.</li> <li>Notification bell \u2014 Alerts and status notifications for your account.</li> <li>Help \u2014 Opens this documentation.</li> <li>Profile avatar \u2014 A circle with your initials on the far right. Select it to open your account menu.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"getting-started/navigation.html#the-profile-menu","title":"The profile menu","text":"<p>Selecting your profile avatar opens a dropdown showing your name and role, followed by these items (each appears only if your role grants access):</p> <ul> <li>Settings \u2014 Personal and workspace settings.</li> <li>Configuration \u2014 Administrative configuration for your site.</li> <li>Log Out \u2014 Ends your session and returns to the sign-in card.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"getting-started/navigation.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>You can move between work areas from the module buttons, reach account options from the profile menu, and always return home via the brand logo.</p> <p>Why your menu may differ</p> <p>The buttons and menu items you see depend on your assigned role and the features enabled for your tenant. Two users on the same site can see different options. If something described here is missing, it is not enabled for your account.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started/navigation.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>The highlighted module button tells you which area you are currently in.</li> <li>Selecting anywhere outside an open dropdown closes it.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"getting-started/navigation.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>First Login</li> <li>Tenants and Locations</li> </ul>"},{"location":"getting-started/tenants-and-locations.html","title":"Tenants and Locations","text":"<p>Two concepts that shape everything you see in DXSuite: the tenant you are working in, and the locations that organize your displays and devices within it.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started/tenants-and-locations.html#what-a-tenant-is","title":"What a tenant is","text":"<p>A tenant is an isolated workspace for a single organization or customer. Each tenant has its own data, users, enabled modules, and features, and one tenant cannot see another tenant's content. When you sign in, you are always working inside one tenant at a time \u2014 its name is shown in the tenant switcher on the top navigation bar.</p> <p>Most users belong to a single tenant and never need to think about this. If your account has access to more than one tenant, you can switch between them.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started/tenants-and-locations.html#what-a-location-is","title":"What a location is","text":"<p>A location represents a physical place or grouping within a tenant \u2014 such as a building, terminal, or site \u2014 under which the displays and the devices that drive them are organized. Locations are how you find and manage a specific set of screens, and how content and access are scoped to the right places.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started/tenants-and-locations.html#steps-switch-tenants","title":"Steps: switch tenants","text":"<ol> <li>Look for the tenant name button on the right side of the top navigation bar. If it is not shown, your account has access to only one tenant and there is nothing to switch.</li> <li>Select the tenant name to open the Switch tenant menu. Your current tenant is marked.</li> <li>Select the tenant you want. DXSuite reloads into that tenant's workspace.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"getting-started/tenants-and-locations.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>After switching, the navigation, modules, and data all reflect the selected tenant. You may be asked to sign in again, because each tenant maintains its own secure session.</p> <p>Modules vary by tenant</p> <p>A tenant only shows the modules and features it is licensed for. If a work area is available in one tenant but not another, that difference is expected.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started/tenants-and-locations.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>To create and manage locations and the displays within them, see Locations.</li> <li>Access to specific locations can be granted per user, so you may see only the locations relevant to your work.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"getting-started/tenants-and-locations.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Locations</li> <li>Navigating DXSuite</li> </ul>"},{"location":"infrastructure/devices.html","title":"Devices","text":"<p>A device is a media player that connects to DXSuite, reports its status, and drives a display by rendering that display's scheduled content on the physical screen.</p> <p>Devices are the bottom level of the DXSuite inventory. Each device registers with Director, sends a regular heartbeat, and \u2014 once assigned to a display \u2014 plays the content scheduled for it. A device must be paired before it can connect.</p>"},{"location":"infrastructure/devices.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>The player must be able to reach your DXSuite server.</li> <li>You must have permission to approve device pairing and manage devices.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"infrastructure/devices.html#pair-a-device","title":"Pair a device","text":"<ol> <li>Start the player. It shows a 6-digit pairing code and appears under pending requests.</li> <li>In Configuration, open Device Pairing.</li> <li>Enter the code in Approve Device and select Approve, or approve the matching entry under Pending Requests.</li> <li>The device receives its token on its next poll and begins connecting.</li> </ol> <p>Warning</p> <p>Selecting Deny on a pairing request blocklists the device. Use Unblock under Denied Devices to let it request pairing again.</p>"},{"location":"infrastructure/devices.html#monitor-devices","title":"Monitor devices","text":"<ol> <li>Open Configuration and select the Devices tab to open Device Management.</li> <li>Each row shows the device and its health, including:<ul> <li>Status \u2014 online or offline, based on whether a recent heartbeat was received.</li> <li>Heartbeat \u2014 how long ago the device last checked in.</li> <li>Content \u2014 content health, shown as Healthy, Unresponsive, or Failed.</li> <li>Identifying details such as Device ID, Model, Serial, and IP Address.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Use the search box to filter, and the Columns control to choose and reorder the columns shown.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"infrastructure/devices.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>A paired, online device that is assigned to a display plays that display's content. Its status and content health appear in Device Management, and you can view its live output under Screenshots.</p>"},{"location":"infrastructure/devices.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Select a device's gear icon to open its settings, where you can enable console logging or flush its local media cache.</li> <li>Click a device's Name or Description cell to edit it inline.</li> <li>Active connections are managed under Device Pairing, where a token can be revoked, reactivated, or deleted.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"infrastructure/devices.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Screenshots</li> <li>Pulse</li> </ul>"},{"location":"infrastructure/displays.html","title":"Displays","text":"<p>A display is a single screen or content surface that belongs to a location and holds its own schedule of content.</p> <p>Displays are the middle level of the DXSuite inventory. A location of a display-driven type (such as Card) can hold one or more displays, and each display is driven by a device \u2014 the player that connects to DXSuite and renders content on the physical screen.</p>"},{"location":"infrastructure/displays.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>A location of a display-driven type must already exist. See Locations.</li> <li>You must have permission to manage locations and displays.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"infrastructure/displays.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":"<p>To add a display to a location:</p> <ol> <li>Open Configuration and select the Locations tab.</li> <li>Find the location and select Edit.</li> <li>In the Displays section, select + Add Display.</li> <li>Enter a Display Name and its Width and Height in pixels.</li> <li>Select Add, then save the location.</li> </ol> <p>To adjust an existing display:</p> <ol> <li>In the Location Management list, expand the location row to reveal its displays.</li> <li>Select Edit on the display. The Edit Display panel opens.</li> <li>Review or change:<ul> <li>Resolution \u2014 the display's width and height.</li> <li>Constraint \u2014 an optional content group or asset library that limits which assets appear in this display's schedule.</li> <li>Workflow \u2014 Schedule for a normal timeline, or Status Selector for button-driven content.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Select Save.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"infrastructure/displays.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>The display appears nested under its location and can be scheduled. Select the display name to open its schedule and manage content.</p> <p>Note</p> <p>A display's resolution should match the physical screen it will drive so that content is sized correctly.</p>"},{"location":"infrastructure/displays.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>A constraint keeps a display's content on-spec by restricting it to assets of a matching resolution.</li> <li>A display must have no assigned device before it can be deleted.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"infrastructure/displays.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Devices</li> <li>Screenshots</li> </ul>"},{"location":"infrastructure/locations.html","title":"Locations","text":"<p>A location is the top level of the DXSuite inventory: it represents a site or content area, holds its displays, and controls how content is created and delivered there.</p> <p>DXSuite organizes your signage estate as a three-level hierarchy:</p> <ul> <li>Location \u2014 a site or content area (for example, a concourse, a lobby, or a shared media pool).</li> <li>Display \u2014 a screen or content surface that belongs to a location. See Displays.</li> <li>Device \u2014 the player that connects to DXSuite and drives a display. See Devices.</li> </ul> <p>Locations are grouped into Location Groups for easier browsing, and each location has a Location Type that determines how it behaves.</p>"},{"location":"infrastructure/locations.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>You must have access to the Configuration area and permission to manage locations.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"infrastructure/locations.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":"<p>To create a location:</p> <ol> <li>Open Configuration and select the Locations tab. The Location Management panel lists existing locations with their group, type, and access.</li> <li>Select + Create Location.</li> <li>Enter a Location Name.</li> <li>Choose a Location Group, or select + Create New Group and enter a name for a new one.</li> <li>Choose a Location Type. Each type is described on the card, for example:<ul> <li>Playlist \u2014 sequential media playback.</li> <li>Card \u2014 multiple displays with scheduling.</li> <li>Layout \u2014 compose multiple locations into zones.</li> <li>Asset Library \u2014 a shared content pool for other locations.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Complete any type-specific settings, then save.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"infrastructure/locations.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>The new location appears in the Location Management list. Location types that drive screens can now hold Displays, and you can expand the row to see them.</p>"},{"location":"infrastructure/locations.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Use the search box to filter the list by location name, group, or type.</li> <li>Select Edit on a location to rename it, set content-source and content-tool options, and (for display-driven types) add displays or content constraints.</li> <li>Select Manage in the Access column to control which users and schedules can reach the location.</li> <li>Deleting a location also removes its displays and their schedules, so confirm carefully.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"infrastructure/locations.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Displays</li> <li>Devices</li> </ul>"},{"location":"pulse/overview.html","title":"Pulse","text":"<p>Pulse is an at-a-glance health surface that summarizes the state of your DXSuite platform on a single dashboard.</p> <p>Where the Devices and Screenshots views focus on individual players, Pulse gives you a high-level read on overall health so you can tell quickly whether everything is running normally.</p>"},{"location":"pulse/overview.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>You must have access to the Pulse dashboard.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"pulse/overview.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>Open the Pulse Dashboard.</li> <li>Review the Pulse Health card. It shows an overall status indicator with a short summary of the current health state.</li> <li>Use the dashboard as your starting point: if the summary looks healthy, no action is needed; if it does not, drill into the affected area.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"pulse/overview.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>You get an immediate sense of platform health from one screen, and a clear signal of when to investigate further.</p> <p>Note</p> <p>Some monitoring detail depends on optional capabilities being enabled in your deployment. If a panel reports that a capability is unavailable, ask your administrator whether it is enabled for your environment.</p>"},{"location":"pulse/overview.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Devices</li> <li>Screenshots</li> </ul>"},{"location":"slc/screenshots.html","title":"Screenshots","text":"<p>The Screenshots view shows live thumbnails captured from your players, so you can confirm at a glance what each screen is actually displaying.</p> <p>Each tile is a recent snapshot from one device. A colored border indicates whether the device has checked in recently, and you can open any tile to browse its snapshot history.</p>"},{"location":"slc/screenshots.html#prerequisites","title":"Prerequisites","text":"<ul> <li>Your players must be reporting screenshots to DXSuite.</li> <li>You must have access to the Screenshots view.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"slc/screenshots.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>Open the Screenshots view (Display View). Tiles load for each reporting player, and a Player Count shows the total.</li> <li>Scan the borders to spot problems:<ul> <li>A green border means the device checked in recently.</li> <li>A red border means the device has not checked in recently and may be offline.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Select a tile to open its Snapshot History.</li> <li>In the history window, set the From and To dates and select Search to load snapshots for that period.</li> <li>Use Previous and Next to step through the snapshots. Each is labelled with its capture time.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"slc/screenshots.html#result","title":"Result","text":"<p>You can visually verify what each screen is showing and review how a screen looked over time to investigate a reported issue.</p> <p>Automatic refresh</p> <p>The Screenshots grid refreshes on its own every few seconds, so the thumbnails stay current without reloading the page.</p> <p>Note</p> <p>A snapshot search covers a limited time range (up to 24 hours per search). Narrow the From and To dates to find a specific moment.</p>"},{"location":"slc/screenshots.html#tips","title":"Tips","text":"<ul> <li>A tile also links to the device's address, so you can open the player directly when it is reachable.</li> <li>Use the red-bordered tiles as a quick triage list, then confirm details in Devices.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"slc/screenshots.html#whats-next","title":"What's next","text":"<ul> <li>Devices</li> <li>Pulse</li> </ul>"},{"location":"troubleshooting/common-issues.html","title":"Common Issues","text":"<p>Symptoms you may run into while using DXSuite, the most likely cause of each, and how to resolve them.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting/common-issues.html#a-user-cant-see-a-location-page-or-module","title":"A user can't see a location, page, or module","text":"<p>Symptom \u2014 A user reports that an expected work area, page, or set of locations is missing from their navigation.</p> <p>Cause \u2014 Access in DXSuite is shaped by the user's role and by their location access. A missing page usually means the role does not grant it; missing locations usually mean the user is scoped to a subset of locations. A whole module may simply not be licensed for the current tenant.</p> <p>Resolution</p> <ol> <li>Open Settings \u2192 Users and confirm the user's Current Role.</li> <li>If the role is correct but a page is still missing, open Settings \u2192 Roles and confirm that role grants the relevant tab, page, or feature permission.</li> <li>Confirm the user has access to the expected locations.</li> <li>If an entire module is absent, verify it is licensed for the tenant. See Tenants and Locations.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"troubleshooting/common-issues.html#a-display-shows-no-content","title":"A display shows no content","text":"<p>Symptom \u2014 A screen is powered on and reachable but shows nothing, or shows an old playlist.</p> <p>Cause \u2014 No content is scheduled or approved for that display right now, or the device has not yet pulled its latest playlist.</p> <p>Resolution</p> <ol> <li>Confirm the display has content assigned and that any schedule covers the current time.</li> <li>Check that the content is approved (see the pending-approval scenario below).</li> <li>Confirm the device is online (see the offline-device scenario below) so it can receive updates.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"troubleshooting/common-issues.html#content-that-was-submitted-isnt-live","title":"Content that was submitted isn't live","text":"<p>Symptom \u2014 New or edited content does not appear on displays even though it was uploaded.</p> <p>Cause \u2014 The content is awaiting approval. Until it is approved, it is not published to displays.</p> <p>Resolution</p> <ol> <li>Have an approver review and approve the pending content.</li> <li>Confirm the submitter's role is expected to route through approval \u2014 only roles with the approval-bypass feature permission skip moderation.</li> <li>After approval, allow devices a short time to pull the update.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"troubleshooting/common-issues.html#a-device-appears-offline","title":"A device appears offline","text":"<p>Symptom \u2014 A device shows as offline in DXSuite and is not updating.</p> <p>Cause \u2014 The device has lost network connectivity or its agent is not running or checking in.</p> <p>Resolution</p> <ol> <li>Confirm the device has power and a working network connection.</li> <li>Restart the device or its player agent and wait for it to check in again.</li> <li>If it remains offline, review its recent status on its detail page to see when it last reported.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"troubleshooting/common-issues.html#signed-out-unexpectedly-session-expired","title":"Signed out unexpectedly / session expired","text":"<p>Symptom \u2014 A user is returned to the sign-in screen mid-task, or is prompted to sign in again after switching tenants.</p> <p>Cause \u2014 Sessions are secured per tenant and time out after a period of inactivity. Switching tenants starts a new secure session.</p> <p>Resolution</p> <ol> <li>Sign in again to resume.</li> <li>Note that after switching tenants you may be asked to re-authenticate \u2014 this is expected. See First Login.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"troubleshooting/common-issues.html#a-support-operator-gets-an-elevation-required-error","title":"A support operator gets an \"elevation required\" error","text":"<p>Symptom \u2014 A platform operator opening a tenant they don't belong to sees an elevation required message, or cannot save changes.</p> <p>Cause \u2014 Operators have no standing access to tenant data. Viewing requires an approved live elevation; changing data additionally requires a Read + Write elevation.</p> <p>Resolution</p> <ol> <li>Request access from Platform \u2192 Elevation and have a second operator approve it.</li> <li>If reads work but writes are blocked, request a Read + Write (TenantSupportWrite) elevation. See Platform Elevation.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"troubleshooting/common-issues.html#the-platform-area-is-missing","title":"The Platform area is missing","text":"<p>Symptom \u2014 An administrator cannot find the Platform navigation group (Tenants, Users, Audit Log, Elevation).</p> <p>Cause \u2014 The Platform area appears only in multi-tenant deployments where a platform database is configured, and only for Platform Operators. Standalone and on-premises installations do not show it.</p> <p>Resolution</p> <ol> <li>Confirm the account is a Platform Operator.</li> <li>Confirm the deployment is multi-tenant with the platform database configured. If it is a standalone install, the Platform area is intentionally absent.</li> </ol>"}]}